{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Found by the evidence pass on ticket sole-curbable-stinkpot (9a71-ba9b-89d0-4cb8). The original sweep believed this string was aborting passes; it is NOT. It is silently swallowed noise, which is its own defect.\n\nObserved in 6 failed runs (31535962079, 31455622724, 31420498173, 31244501098, 31129551929, 31129431096), 15-21 occurrences per run:\n\n    ACLI stderr: X Error: command cancelled\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe outbound link-apply loop swallows every ACLI link failure, and one arm actively\nmis-reports failure as success. Operators reading `RECON: batch_outcome ... error=None\nlinks_applied=N` cannot tell a real apply from a cancelled one.\n\nPremise validated in code (2026-08-12):\n\n- `_run_acli` (`acli_subprocess.py`) prints `ACLI stderr: ...` only after ALL 3 attempts are\n  exhausted, with 2s + 4s backoff. That ~6s cost per exhausted cycle matches the observed\n  ~6.3s spacing exactly. So each logged line is one fully-retried, fully-failed ACLI call.\n- `_update_one_dispatch_links` (`dispatch_one.py`) then swallows the raise. `set_relationship`\n  failures hit `except Exception` -> `print(...)` only. `delete_issue_link` failures hit\n  `except subprocess.CalledProcessError` which **increments `_links_applied`** — a failed\n  delete is counted as an applied link.\n- Nothing propagates into `outcome[\"error\"]`, so `applier.py` prints a success-shaped line.\n- The `REBAR_RECONCILER_FAIL_SILENT_NOOP` canary (`apply_handlers.py`) tests\n  `computed > 0 and applied == 0`. The `CalledProcessError` arm inflates `applied`, so\n  `computed == applied` and the canary can never fire for link deletes.\n\n## Approach\n\nMirror change 1640's fast-abort pattern (tight stderr marker + typed error), then stop the\nmis-count and surface the truth in the outcome. Three cohesive edits:\n\n1. **Make cancellation loud and distinct** (`acli_subprocess.py`). Add a tight\n   `command cancelled` stderr marker and a typed `AcliCancelledError(RuntimeError)` carrying\n   cmd + stderr. `_run_acli` fast-aborts on the FIRST attempt when it matches: an EOF-on-stdin\n   confirmation prompt is deterministic, so the 2 retries and their 6s of backoff are pure\n   waste. Subclassing `RuntimeError` keeps existing `except RuntimeError` sites unaffected,\n   exactly as 1640 did for `AcliAuthError`.\n\n2. **Stop counting failed deletes as applied** (`dispatch_one.py`). The `CalledProcessError`\n   arm keeps its idempotent rationale but narrows it: it counts toward `_links_applied` only\n   when stderr matches an idempotent-outcome marker (`404`, `409`, `does not exist`,\n   `not found`). Every other failure — including `command cancelled` — increments a new\n   `_links_failed` instead. `set_relationship` failures also increment `_links_failed`.\n\n3. **Surface it** (`dispatch_one.py` -> `apply_handlers.py` -> `applier.py`). `links_failed`\n   rides the existing `subop` dict onto the outcome and is printed in the `batch_outcome`\n   line, so a cancelled link is visible in the same log an operator already reads.\n\n**Deliberate non-goal (default decision, not a question).** This change does NOT add `--yes`\nto `workitem link create` / `link delete`. That is the ticket's stated lead for the *root*\ncause, but it is a live behaviour change that cannot be verified offline, and story 25ae\nalready proved the installed ACLI rejects `--json` on those same subcommands — passing an\nunaccepted flag would break every link op in the fleet. This ticket is scoped to reliability\nand observability: make the failure loud, stop the lie. A follow-up ticket is filed\n(`discovered_from` this one) to probe `--yes` acceptance against live ACLI and add it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py` — cancellation marker,\n  `AcliCancelledError`, fast-abort in `_run_acli`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` — `_links_failed` counter; narrow the\n  `CalledProcessError` idempotent arm; count create failures.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_handlers.py` — carry `links_failed` onto outcome.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/applier.py` — print `links_failed` in `batch_outcome`.\n- `tests/` — new tests for the above.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: a link create + delete that both succeed still report\n  `links_applied=2 links_failed=0`, and `batch_outcome` is unchanged in shape.\n- Failure path: `_run_acli` fed a `command cancelled` stderr raises `AcliCancelledError` on\n  the FIRST attempt — asserted by counting subprocess spawns == 1 (no retry, no backoff).\n- Mis-count regression: a `delete_issue_link` raising `CalledProcessError` with\n  `command cancelled` stderr yields `links_applied=0 links_failed=1`, NOT the current\n  `links_applied=1`.\n- Idempotent path preserved: the same failure with `404` stderr still counts as applied and\n  does not increment `links_failed` (no new hard failure on benign concurrent removal).\n- Edge: `set_relationship` failure increments `links_failed` and the pass stays non-fatal\n  (the loop does not unwind).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `AcliCancelledError` exists in `acli_subprocess.py` and `_run_acli` raises it on the\n      first attempt when stderr matches the `command cancelled` marker — verified by a test\n      that asserts exactly one subprocess spawn.\n- [ ] The cancellation marker is tight enough not to match unrelated stderr — verified by a\n      test asserting a `cancelled the request by policy`-style unrelated string does NOT raise\n      `AcliCancelledError`.\n- [ ] A failed `delete_issue_link` whose stderr is NOT an idempotent marker no longer\n      increments `links_applied` — verified by a test asserting\n      `links_applied=0, links_failed=1`.\n- [ ] A failed `delete_issue_link` whose stderr IS an idempotent marker (404/409) still\n      counts as applied — verified by a test asserting `links_applied=1, links_failed=0`.\n- [ ] The `RECON: batch_outcome` line for an `update` includes `links_failed=<n>` — verified\n      by a test capturing stderr and asserting the substring.\n- [ ] A follow-up ticket probing `--yes` acceptance on `link create`/`link delete` exists and\n      is linked `discovered_from` this ticket — verified by `rebar show` on this ticket\n      listing the inbound dep.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, and the new tests pass under `pytest`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786524311190425001,"uuid":"8a356fe9-f3bf-428f-a650-6b13cac01d84"}